ormula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 29}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-5(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +29) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 29}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269079 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i} \\\\ -7+4\\text{i} \\\\ -6+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2-6\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i} \\\\ -6+\\text{i} \\\\ -5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-7\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2-4\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i} \\\\ -7+4\\text{i} \\\\ -6+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-6\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i} \\\\ -6+\\text{i} \\\\ -5-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-7\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2-4\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269079 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269080 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 5-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4-7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5+\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 5-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4-7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 3+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5+\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269080 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269081 :question/problem "$[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186269081 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot 3+2\\cdot -2 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+5\\cdot -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7\\cdot 3+2\\cdot -2 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+5\\cdot -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269082 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each week: \n\n- $35%$ of children who preferred ice creams swap to cakes. This implies that $65%$ of them continue to prefer ice creams.\n\n- $42%$ of children who preferred cakes swap to ice creams. This implies that $58%$ of them continue to prefer cakes.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.65$ | $0.42$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.35$ | $0.58$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.65 & 0.42 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.58\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We are given the following situation for each week: \n\n- $35%$ of children who preferred ice creams swap to cakes. This implies that $65%$ of them continue to prefer ice creams.\n\n- $42%$ of children who preferred cakes swap to ice creams. This implies that $58%$ of them continue to prefer cakes.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.65$ | $0.42$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.35$ | $0.58$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.65 & 0.42 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.58\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269083 :question/problem "A study shows that for a particular area in a specific season,\n\n- on a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & b & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "A study shows that for a particular area in a specific season,\n\n- on a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & b & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269083 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for this area:\n\n- On a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $40%$ chance of not having precipitation on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $55%$ chance of having rain on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain. This implies a $45%$ chance of having snow on this day.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **No Precipitation** | **Rain** | **Snow** |\n| To: | **No Precipitation** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| **Rain** | $0.25$ | $0.55$ | $0.05$ |  |\n| **Snow** | $0.35$ | $0.05$ | $0.45$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.55 & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & 0.45\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.4+0.55+0.45=1.4.\n$$" "We are given the following situation for this area:\n\n- On a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $40%$ chance of not having precipitation on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $55%$ chance of having rain on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain. This implies a $45%$ chance of having snow on this day.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **No Precipitation** | **Rain** | **Snow** |\n| To: | **No Precipitation** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| **Rain** | $0.25$ | $0.55$ | $0.05$ |  |\n| **Snow** | $0.35$ | $0.05$ | $0.45$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.55 & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & 0.45\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.4+0.55+0.45=1.4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269084 :question/problem "There are three cable TV providers in a particular apartment block: provider $A,$ provider $B,$ and provider $C.$ A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & a & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & b \\\\ c & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "There are three cable TV providers in a particular apartment block: provider $A,$ provider $B,$ and provider $C.$ A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & a & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & b \\\\ c & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269084 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C.$  This implies that $10%$ of them continue using provider $A.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue using provider $B.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$ This implies that $20%$ of them continue using provider $C.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Provider A** | **Provider B** | **Provider C** |\n| To: | **Provider A** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Provider B** | $0.8$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |  |\n| **Provider C** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.25 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.1+0.1=0.45.\n$$" "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C.$  This implies that $10%$ of them continue using provider $A.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue using provider $B.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$ This implies that $20%$ of them continue using provider $C.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Provider A** | **Provider B** | **Provider C** |\n| To: | **Provider A** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Provider B** | $0.8$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |  |\n| **Provider C** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.25 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.1+0.1=0.45.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269085 :question/problem "Find $Hn$ if \n$H=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$n=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find $Hn$ if \n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$n=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186269085 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot -3+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 2\\cdot -3+(-5)\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Hn=[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-7)\\cdot -3+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 2\\cdot -3+(-5)\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Hn=\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186269086 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269086 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269087 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2+7\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-7\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2+7\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269087 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269105 :question/problem "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186269105 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 5\\cdot 5+(-3)\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 5\\cdot 5+(-3)\\cdot 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269133 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269133 :question/worked-solution "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+(-2)=48.\n$$" "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+(-2)=48.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269134 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & a \\\\ c & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ b \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}40 & -20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & a \\\\ c & 30 & 90\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ b \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269134 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & 20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & 30 \\\\ -60 & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=30+5+(-60)=-25.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}40 & 20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & 30 \\\\ -60 & 30 & 90\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=30+5+(-60)=-25.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269135 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-3\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}20 & a & 10 \\\\ c & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ b \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-3\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}20 & a & 10 \\\\ c & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ b \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269135 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}20 & -40 & 10 \\\\ -40 & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ -44 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\na-b+c=(-40)-(-44)+(-40)=-36.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}20 & -40 & 10 \\\\ -40 & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ -44 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\na-b+c=(-40)-(-44)+(-40)=-36.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269136 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\\,{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269136 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot 0+(-1)=0.\n$$" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot 0+(-1)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269137 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269137 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269138 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269138 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ -14 & 14 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4a+\\frac{b}{c}=4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{1}=3.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}14 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ -14 & 14 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4a+\\frac{b}{c}=4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{1}=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269139 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-\\frac{3b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-\\frac{3b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269139 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 36\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 18 & 36 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-\\frac{3b}{c}=-1-\\frac{3(-2)}{1}=5.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 36\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 18 & 36 & -18\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-\\frac{3b}{c}=-1-\\frac{3(-2)}{1}=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269140 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269140 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 27\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ -27 & 27 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-9a+\\frac{b}{c}=-9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{1}{1}=4.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}27 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 27\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ -27 & 27 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-9a+\\frac{b}{c}=-9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{1}{1}=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269141 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $7a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $7a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269141 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ -28 & 56 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n7a+\\frac{b}{c}=7\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{7})+\\frac{2}{1}=0.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ -28 & 56 & 8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n7a+\\frac{b}{c}=7\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{7})+\\frac{2}{1}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269143 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries *below* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, it is an upper triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $B$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry below the diagonal:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, they are not upper triangular matrices." "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries *below* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence, it is an upper triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $B$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry below the diagonal:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, they are not upper triangular matrices."] [:db/cas 17592186269144 :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269144 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix." "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186269145 :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269145 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is an upper triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not a lower triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element above the diagonal: ${a}_{12}=1\\ne 0.$" "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is an upper triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not a lower triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element above the diagonal: ${a}_{12}=1\\ne 0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186269146 :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is upper triangular\n\n- **II.** $A$ is lower triangular\n\n- **III.** ${A}^{T}+A$ is lower triangular" "Consider the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is upper triangular\n\n- **II.** $A$ is lower triangular\n\n- **III.** ${A}^{T}+A$ is lower triangular"] [:db/cas 17592186269146 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since all the entries below the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true, since the result of $A+{A}^{T}$ has all the entries above the main diagonal equal to zero: \n\n$$\n{A}^{T}+A=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since all the entries below the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true, since the result of $A+{A}^{T}$ has all the entries above the main diagonal equal to zero: \n\n$$\n{A}^{T}+A=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."] [:db/cas 17592186269148 :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269148 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix." "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186269149 :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269149 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a lower triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not an upper triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element below the diagonal: ${a}_{21}=2\\ne 0.$" "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a lower triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not an upper triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element below the diagonal: ${a}_{21}=2\\ne 0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186269151 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186269151 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}37 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}55 \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=37+17+55=109.\n$$" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}37 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}55 \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=37+17+55=109.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269152 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 